<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sabuktigin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 942–<a href="/years/997">997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Sebüktigin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sabuktigin was a Turkic slave commander who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> from 977 to 997, nominally for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, and founded the Ghaznavid dynasty. He extended his power over Bust and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> valley and repeatedly defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Shahi king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapala">Jayapala</a>, pushing his frontier towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> built the dynasty into an empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabuktigin.jpg" alt="Sabuktigin" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Ghaznavid_amir_Sabuktigin,_citing_the_Samanid_amir_Nuh_II_as_his_overlord._Date_unknown,_minted_at_Ghazna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sabuktigin was a Karluk Turkic slave. In his own Pandnama, a mirror for princes, he said he came from a tribe in Barskhan. His tribe was raided, and he was captured and sold at a slave market at Nakhshab, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarshi">Qarshi</a>. He was bought by Alp-Tegin, himself a slave and a prominent commander who headed the Samanid royal guard. In 962 Alp-Tegin fell from grace and set out to establish an independent rule in Ghazna, and Sabuktigin went with him. Alp-Tegin took Ghazna from its local ruler Abu Bakr Lawik, was recognised as governor by the Samanid administration, and died in 963.</p><p>After the brief reign of Alp-Tegin's son Abu Ishaq Ibrahim, who died in 966, the Turkic ghulams of Ghazna reconciled with the Samanid government but chose their own leaders from among their commanders. In 977 the citizens of Ghazna invited Abu Ali Lawik to rule them, and the Hindu Shahi dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> supported him with a large force. Sabuktigin defeated the invaders at Charkh and killed Lawik. With the army's support he replaced Böritigin as governor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 978 Sabuktigin invaded Rukkhaj and Bust and then Qusdar in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Baluchistan</a>, together with a number of Shahi frontier forts. The Shahi maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapala">Jayapala</a> allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and marched on Ghazna in 986. The battle at Lamghan had no clear winner for days until a snowstorm devastated Jayapala's army; the historian al-Utbi credited the storm to Sabuktigin himself. In 988 Sabuktigin marched against him with an army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalaj">Khalajs</a>. Jayapala gathered help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, Kalinjar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, yet was beaten at Lamghan, and Sabuktigin took the lands between Lamghan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, garrisoning Peshawar with 2,000 horsemen.</p><p>He acknowledged Samanid sovereignty throughout his reign, minting the Samanid amirs' names before his own, though this was nominal. In 994 the Samanid amir Nuh II asked for help against the rebel Abu Ali Simjuri and his Kara-Khanid ally Fa'iq Khassa. Sabuktigin and his son Mahmud met them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and won after the Ziyarid prince Dara deserted Abu Ali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sabuktigin died in a village north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> in August or September 997, on his way back to Ghazna. After a succession struggle, Mahmud deposed his brother Isma'il in 998. Ghaznavid historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-bayhaqi">Abu'l-Fadl Bayhaqi</a> portrayed Sabuktigin as a just ruler, but the historian Clifford Edmund Bosworth holds that no traits of his personality can be known from the evidence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-biruni">Al-Biruni</a> said that he opened the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for Mahmud.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sabuktigin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After several short-lived successors, the Turkic garrison chose another former slave soldier, <b>Sabuktigin</b>, as its leader in 977.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They kept a court in Guzgan and married into the family of the Ghaznavid ruler <b>Sabuktigin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/farighunid-dynasty">Farighunid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghaznavid Empire launched repeated invasions: Jayapala was defeated by <b>Sabuktigin</b> and then by Mahmud of Ghazni in 1001 at Peshawar, after which he committed suicide.</blockquote><a href="/state/hindu-shahis">Hindu Shahis</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Barskoon, in what is now <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.16,77.6&amp;year=977" aria-label="Barskoon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6434 664 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sabuktigin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6884 934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6884 934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.16,77.6&amp;year=977">What was here in 977 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
